dax: an invalidated entry's pages are unmapped under its lock

invalidate_inode_pages2_range puts each clean entry's folio, then
unmaps the range: nothing keeps faults out meanwhile, as a truncate's
invalidate lock does. A PMD read fault holding its entry, which a write
saying its pages are new waits for, maps its PMD and lets the entry go;
the write's invalidation then puts the folio while the PMD maps it,
splitting it under the PMD (dax_folio_put's WARN), and the PMD names a
folio no entry has until the unmap after. Each entry's pages are
unmapped first, the entry locked so that no fault maps them again, as
grab_mapping_entry does a PMD it downgrades. Found by fxdax's guest/sim
(fxdax TASK-150.21).

diff --git a/fs/dax.c b/fs/dax.c
--- a/fs/dax.c
+++ b/fs/dax.c
@@ -831,6 +831,23 @@ static int __dax_invalidate_entry(struct address_space *mapping,
 	    (xas_get_mark(&xas, PAGECACHE_TAG_DIRTY) ||
 	     xas_get_mark(&xas, PAGECACHE_TAG_TOWRITE)))
 		goto out;
+	/*
+	 * Nothing keeps faults out of an invalidation, as a truncate's lock
+	 * does: one may have mapped the entry since the caller unmapped its
+	 * range, or the caller unmaps it only after. Its pages are unmapped
+	 * now, the entry locked so that no fault maps them again, before its
+	 * folio goes: one still mapped is split under its PMD (the WARN in
+	 * dax_folio_put), and named by no entry.
+	 */
+	if (!trunc && !dax_is_empty_entry(entry)) {
+		unsigned long nr = 1UL << dax_entry_order(entry);
+
+		dax_lock_entry(&xas, entry);
+		xas_unlock_irq(&xas);
+		unmap_mapping_pages(mapping, index & ~(nr - 1), nr, false);
+		xas_reset(&xas);
+		xas_lock_irq(&xas);
+	}
 	dax_disassociate_entry(entry, mapping, trunc);
 	xas_store(&xas, NULL);
 	mapping->nrpages -= 1UL << dax_entry_order(entry);
